00:08:09.190 --> 00:08:13.280
(rock music)

00:08:13.280 --> 00:08:18.880
The date was April 12th, 1997. The venue:&nbsp;
the Rialto Theatre in Cincinnati, Ohio.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:22.480
This is where the 10PM sneak preview&nbsp;
screening of the new movie Stab would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:22.480 --> 00:08:27.040
take place. Sunrise Studios distributed free&nbsp;
tickets and even handed out Ghostface costumes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:32.320
to each of the attendees. The atmosphere was&nbsp;
absolutely ****ing electric. Movie patrons&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:36.960
chased each other around the auditorium as cloaked&nbsp;
Ghostface decor flew above the excited audience.

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:41.600
Unfortunately for the moviegoers, disaster was&nbsp;
not limited to the horrors that they saw on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.600 --> 00:08:46.160
silver screen. Approximately five minutes into&nbsp;
the film, a college student named Phil Stevens&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:46.160 --> 00:08:50.480
stepped into the bathroom, where he was stabbed&nbsp;
in the face and killed by a new Ghostface killer.

00:08:50.480 --> 00:08:55.200
Like Sidney and Billy, he too was connected&nbsp;
to Sunrise Studios. Phil Stevens is the son of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.200 --> 00:09:00.960
the studio’s commissary chef, Martin Stevens. So&nbsp;
again, I have to ask: could this be a coincidence?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.960 --> 00:09:05.600
Like my answer about Maureen and Henry, I think&nbsp;
his connection to the studio is only tangential.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:10.240
The killers this time around were Windsor College&nbsp;
student Mickey Altieri and Billy Loomis’s mother,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.240 --> 00:09:13.920
Debbie. Debbie wasn’t married to Henry&nbsp;
Loomis at the time he worked at Sunrise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.920 --> 00:09:18.560
so I don’t think she’d have any way of knowing&nbsp;
who was and was not related to a Sunrise employee.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.560 --> 00:09:22.000
It would also later be discovered that the&nbsp;
victims were chosen specifically, not because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.000 --> 00:09:25.760
of any Hollywood blood connection, but rather&nbsp;
because the killers were trying to recreate the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.760 --> 00:09:29.760
killings in Woodsboro by using people with&nbsp;
similar names. So in the end, I think that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.760 --> 00:09:33.440
Phil was probably at the screening because&nbsp;
he got the free tickets from his dad. Maybe&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:37.600
his interest in horror movies even spawned from&nbsp;
having a family member work at the movie studio.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.600 --> 00:09:42.160
But it’s unlikely that that family connection has&nbsp;
anything to do with why he was chosen as a victim.

00:09:42.160 --> 00:09:44.640
The killer at the theater,&nbsp;
which was most likely Mickey,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:44.640 --> 00:09:47.520
since he goes to college with the&nbsp;
victims and has been known to carry&nbsp;&nbsp;around

00:09:47.520 --> 00:09:51.040
a camcorder, steals Phil’s jacket&nbsp;
and returns to his seat in the auditorium,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.040 --> 00:09:56.240
where he then stabs Phil’s girlfriend Maureen, who&nbsp;
dies very publicly in front of the movie audience.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:09:58.320
This incident gets a ton of coverage in the press,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.320 --> 00:10:01.840
and the studio discusses the possibility
of even pulling the movie, although,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.840 --> 00:10:06.560
this was most likely just virtue signaling; in&nbsp;
reality they likely had no intention of doing so.

00:10:06.560 --> 00:10:08.960
– It’d be stupid to pull this movie. With all&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.960 --> 00:10:11.600
this free press they’re gonna&nbsp;
have huge numbers this weekend.

00:10:11.600 --> 00:10:15.440
– The incident fueled a cultural discussion about&nbsp;
if the movie could have been responsible for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:15.440 --> 00:10:19.840
the crimes that occured in the theater. So in&nbsp;
actuality, they promote the movie even harder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:19.840 --> 00:10:23.680
sending the star, Tori Spelling on&nbsp;
television to be interviewed by Nancy O’Dell.

00:10:24.840 --> 00:10:28.880
– So tell us about this part&nbsp;
you’re getting rave reviews for.&nbsp;

00:10:28.880 --> 00:10:32.560
– Well I play this young girl, Sidney&nbsp;
Prescott, who discovers that her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:37.120
boyfriend’s this crazy serial killer, who&nbsp;
also killed her mother the year before that.&nbsp;

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:39.360
– So what kind of research&nbsp;
did you do for the part?

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:43.120
– But during this theatrical run, the events&nbsp;
of the next Stab film were playing out in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:43.120 --> 00:10:47.120
real life at Windsor College, where the&nbsp;
real Sidney Prescott was taking classes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:50.000
Once again, Gale Weathers showed&nbsp;
up to report on the new story.

00:10:50.000 --> 00:10:53.840
Sorority girl Cici Cooper was stabbed&nbsp;
and thrown over her balcony. Sidney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:57.680
herself is attacked that same night,&nbsp;
but manages to survive. The next day,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.680 --> 00:11:02.720
Randy is not so lucky. Mickey manages to take&nbsp;
out Sidney’s two security details, her roommate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.720 --> 00:11:07.040
and her boyfriend before Sidney and Cotton&nbsp;
manage to get the better of him and Mrs. Loomis.

00:11:07.040 --> 00:11:09.200
While Mrs. Loomis had only been out for revenge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:13.840
Mickey’s motive is significant to the Stab&nbsp;
franchise. He actually wanted to get caught.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:18.800
– [Mickey] I’ve got my whole defense planned&nbsp;
out. I’m gonna blame the movies.

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:22.720
– For him, it was all about the trial and the&nbsp;
entertainment and attention that comes with it.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:22.720 --> 00:11:26.720
It’s likely that he was jealous of all the&nbsp;
extra notoriety that former NFL player OJ&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:31.280
Simpson gained during his murder trial, which&nbsp;
occured a couple years beforehard in 1995.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.280 --> 00:11:36.160
According to Forbes, “More than 150 million&nbsp;
viewers, 57% of the country, tuned in to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.160 --> 00:11:41.280
watch the live broadcast of the verdict at&nbsp;
10 a.m. on Oct. 3, 1995.” For comparison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.280 --> 00:11:46.240
that’s more than that year’s Super Bowl, which&nbsp;
drew in 125 million. News stations covered OJ&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.240 --> 00:11:50.640
continuously throughout the year, so Mickey&nbsp;
probably envisioned himself in that spotlight.

00:11:50.640 --> 00:11:53.760
Those plans were foiled when he and&nbsp;
Mrs. Loomis were killed that evening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.760 --> 00:11:58.080
so he never got his big trial, but he would&nbsp;
still receive a lot posthumous media attention&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.080 --> 00:12:01.360
as the villain in Gale Weathers’s new&nbsp;
book: The Windsor College Murders.

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:05.200
Per Gale’s website: “Ms. Weathers is once&nbsp;
again in the eye of a dangerous storm and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:08.720
emerges a hero. This time, murder&nbsp;
follows Sidney Prescott to college,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.720 --> 00:12:12.320
where Ms. Weathers is covering a story on&nbsp;
the copycat murders of two students during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:15.760
a screening of the hit slasher film.&nbsp;
Read how Gale single handedly brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.760 --> 00:12:19.840
the killers to justice in this exciting&nbsp;
novel that became the hit movie Stab 2.”

00:12:19.840 --> 00:12:22.880
That description might be slightly&nbsp;
embellished, considering she was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.880 --> 00:12:25.840
actually passed out in an orchestra pit&nbsp;
when the killers were brought to justice,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:25.840 --> 00:12:29.440
but I’m sure Sidney wouldn’t mind having&nbsp;
the spotlight shifted away from her.

00:12:29.440 --> 00:12:33.200
It was probably around this time when John&nbsp;
Milton greenlighted the production of Stab 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.200 --> 00:12:37.280
but before that would happen, there was still&nbsp;
the home-video release of Stab 1 to worry about.

00:12:37.280 --> 00:12:41.040
Stab is listed as having a 111&nbsp;
minute runtime, same as Scream,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.040 --> 00:12:44.800
though it’s not known if that refers to&nbsp;
the theatrical cut or the home media cut.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.800 --> 00:12:48.960
I’m not calling it the extended cut, because just&nbsp;
based on the opening scene where Casey is killed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.960 --> 00:12:51.920
it seems that the theatrical&nbsp;
cut is nearly 4 minutes longer.

00:12:51.920 --> 00:12:54.080
The biggest difference is&nbsp;
probably the music. In the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.080 --> 00:12:56.560
theatrical cut we hear the song Red Right Hand…

00:12:56.560 --> 00:13:03.771
🎵"Red Right Hand" - Nick Cave and the Bad Seeds 🎵

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:06.800
…whereas the home-release cut&nbsp;
contains these haunting strings.

00:13:06.800 --> 00:13:11.280
(spooky music)

00:13:11.280 --> 00:13:15.440
The out-of-universe explanation for this is&nbsp;
that Scre4m is the only film not to contain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:15.440 --> 00:13:20.320
the song "Red Right Hand" at all. According to&nbsp;
scream.fandom.com this is because of a copyright&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.320 --> 00:13:24.480
issue, though they don’t link any evidence to&nbsp;
back that up. I’m guessing they’re trying to say&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.480 --> 00:13:28.800
Scream 4 couldn’t afford the licensing fee. The&nbsp;
fourth movie has the same budget as the third,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:28.800 --> 00:13:32.640
but if you adjust both for inflation,&nbsp;
the fourth is actually 30% lower.

00:13:32.640 --> 00:13:36.880
This could also serve as an in-universe&nbsp;
explanation. Maybe the studio had the budget&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:41.760
for "Red Right Hand" for the theatrical rights only.&nbsp;
And this really happens from time to time. To use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:46.960
another example from 1997, the MTV show Daria&nbsp;
first aired with music from popular artists,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.960 --> 00:13:50.720
but it had to be replaced with stock&nbsp;
music for the VHS and DVD releases.

00:13:50.720 --> 00:13:53.360
I’ve lined both cuts of Stab&nbsp;
up by all their common points,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:53.360 --> 00:13:56.720
and I’m going to do a full comparison of&nbsp;
the two. The theatrical cut from Scream&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:56.720 --> 00:14:00.720
2 is on the left and the home media cut&nbsp;
from Scre4m is on the right. But first,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:04.480
there’s one thing I want to highlight&nbsp;
that neither of these shows: the previews.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:04.480 --> 00:14:08.317
Because nobody wants to be advertised to while&nbsp;
they’re in the middle of watching something.

00:14:08.317 --> 00:14:09.677
(impact)

00:14:13.390 --> 00:14:17.244
(horror drone)

00:14:17.244 --> 00:14:20.160
OK, so whenever one of the cuts has&nbsp;
added content, I’ll just pause the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:20.160 --> 00:14:23.840
opposite version. And if you don’t care about&nbsp;
the miniscule differences between the cuts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.840 --> 00:14:27.600
feel free to just skip to the next chapter&nbsp;
of this video. The fourth Scream shows this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:27.600 --> 00:14:31.840
Robert Rodriguez title card, which we don’t&nbsp;
see in Scream 2, but it probably did happen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.840 --> 00:14:34.960
before Phil and Maureen walked into&nbsp;
the theater. Then we’re in sync…

00:14:35.518 --> 00:14:38.961
(knives slashing and music)

00:14:38.961 --> 00:14:40.961
(crowd cheers)

00:14:42.240 --> 00:14:46.080
…until this part, there’s an added scene in the&nbsp;
home release, but we don’t see it. We’ll fast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:46.080 --> 00:14:49.280
forward through the parts where neither Stab&nbsp;
is shown until the Gale Weathers title card.

00:14:52.960 --> 00:14:56.533
Then we’ve got more of neither Stab being shown,&nbsp;
but there are some nice parallels. Then there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:56.533 --> 00:15:00.480
is a section where there’s added content on the&nbsp;
Blu-Ray, but again, we don’t see it until here,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:00.480 --> 00:15:05.120
and it’s just a black screen. Very exciting. But&nbsp;
it leads into the fade-in to the Becker house.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:05.120 --> 00:15:09.280
There are four extra seconds in the theatrical&nbsp;
cut which we don’t see… followed by five extra&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.280 --> 00:15:12.800
seconds of Casey entering the bathroom and&nbsp;
turning on the light. We’re back in sync.

00:15:14.284 --> 00:15:16.284
(crowd jeering)

00:15:17.831 --> 00:15:20.628
– [Gale] Ooh yeah. Back on my game.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:25.680
– [Zac] Then there’s a long added portion in the&nbsp;
home-release cut, a minute and 38, but we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:25.680 --> 00:15:30.240
don’t see any of it because Scream 4 is focused&nbsp;
on Gale, before finally Casey turns on the shower.

00:15:32.880 --> 00:15:34.320
And you’ll notice some of these bathroom shots&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.320 --> 00:15:38.044
that linger a little bit are&nbsp;
cut down for the home release.

00:15:38.044 --> 00:15:40.794
(spooky music, cheering crowd)

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:44.480
Audiences are just as excited about seeing&nbsp;
Heather Graham’s back in 2010 as they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:44.480 --> 00:15:48.960
back in 1997. Then we don’t see either Stab so&nbsp;
we can fast forward here, where we still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:48.960 --> 00:15:53.280
don’t see either screen yet, but there are 4-5&nbsp;
seconds cut of the home release. We actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.280 --> 00:15:57.360
see slightly more of the shot of Casey turning&nbsp;
around in Scream 4, which makes me wonder if the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.360 --> 00:16:01.520
full cut is sitting around somewhere. I’ll do a&nbsp;
short fast forward to Casey walking to the phone.

00:16:01.520 --> 00:16:04.696
(phone ringing)

00:16:05.280 --> 00:16:09.916
Then there’s a second added to home release, to&nbsp;
put us back in sync for Casey answering the phone.

00:16:09.916 --> 00:16:11.916
– Hello?

00:16:11.916 --> 00:16:12.889
– [caller] Hello.

00:16:13.510 --> 00:16:14.400
– Who is this?

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:18.000
– [Zac] There are big chunks of the conversation&nbsp;
only present in the theatrical cut. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:18.000 --> 00:16:20.988
wonder if this is considered lost&nbsp;
media in the Scream universe.

00:16:20.988 --> 00:16:23.517
– [caller] Were you expecting somebody?

00:16:23.517 --> 00:16:26.800
– Home release must be the definitive&nbsp;
version, because everybody has it memorized.

00:16:26.998 --> 00:16:28.400
– No.

00:16:28.400 --> 00:16:31.920
– [Zac] And then the whole section of Casey walking around&nbsp;
the house and talking to Ghostface was actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.920 --> 00:16:36.240
cut out of the home release as well. Which is&nbsp;
a shame because we miss this hilarious line.

00:16:36.839 --> 00:16:38.839
– [Casey] I don't even have a boyfriend right now.

00:16:38.952 --> 00:16:42.000
– [caller] Would you liiikeeee oneee??? Hmm?

00:16:42.160 --> 00:16:45.760
– [Zac] This section is another portion that was removed&nbsp;
from the home release. On the Scream 2 side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:45.760 --> 00:16:49.680
we don’t see Stab, but we do hear Ghostface&nbsp;
taunting her. Nothing’s really happening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.680 --> 00:16:52.400
I can kind of see why they cut&nbsp;
this, so I’ll fast forward again to…

00:16:52.710 --> 00:16:56.240
– You know I don't even know you, and I dislike you already!

00:16:56.240 --> 00:16:59.440
– [Zac] Then it’s back to seeing neither Stab,&nbsp;
so I will fly through this and note that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:59.440 --> 00:17:02.320
this includes another huge chunk of the&nbsp;
theatrical cut that was removed from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:06.480
home release. The cut portion is one minute&nbsp;
and 42 seconds and we don’t see any of it.

00:17:06.942 --> 00:17:07.942
(glass shatters)

00:17:08.462 --> 00:17:09.077
(screams)

00:17:11.040 --> 00:17:14.198
Then, these 5 seconds are only&nbsp;
present in the home release.

00:17:19.600 --> 00:17:21.920
The next part may or may not&nbsp;
be in sync, we don’t know,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:21.920 --> 00:17:25.920
because we only see a tiny glimpse of the&nbsp;
screen in Scream 2, so I'm fast forwarding.

00:17:25.920 --> 00:17:28.880
This is probably in both cuts, we&nbsp;
only see it in Scream 2 though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.880 --> 00:17:31.840
so I just figured I’d show you this in&nbsp;
real time, because we’re about to...

00:17:31.840 --> 00:17:34.800
Fast forward! To a shot you can&nbsp;
only see in the home release.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:34.800 --> 00:17:38.320
We skip forward again landing us in another big&nbsp;
portion that was removed before the home release,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:38.320 --> 00:17:43.475
this one lasts 2 minutes and 23 seconds&nbsp;
overall, part of which includes this shot:

00:17:43.662 --> 00:17:48.000
(intense music)

00:17:48.858 --> 00:17:50.253
And… this shot.

00:17:50.253 --> 00:17:55.817
(intense music)

00:17:56.125 --> 00:17:57.525
And… this shot.

00:17:57.732 --> 00:17:59.725
(intense music)

00:17:59.725 --> 00:18:01.064
And these three shots…

00:18:01.064 --> 00:18:03.412
(suspenseful music)

00:18:03.412 --> 00:18:05.681
And who could forget these?

00:18:06.097 --> 00:18:08.712
(dramatic music and screaming crowd)

00:18:09.850 --> 00:18:10.804
(fast forward)

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:13.058
(flesh stab)

00:18:13.271 --> 00:18:14.622
(blood curdling scream)

00:18:14.882 --> 00:18:15.834
(moan)

00:18:15.834 --> 00:18:18.480
It’s likely that some of these shots&nbsp;
were condensed and included somewhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:18.480 --> 00:18:20.640
in the home release, during&nbsp;
one of the off-screen portions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:22.960
because they’re kind of important&nbsp;
to get us from point A to point B.

00:18:23.280 --> 00:18:26.720
Then this next part is weird, we&nbsp;
actually cut back to Stab in both movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:26.720 --> 00:18:29.360
first in Scream 4, then moments later in Scream 2,

00:18:29.360 --> 00:18:32.800
So I had to pause Scream 4 because&nbsp;
they’re showing different shots entirely.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:34.960
Obviously things don’t match&nbsp;
up between the two because of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:34.960 --> 00:18:38.160
the different shot being used. But&nbsp;
then we fast forward again up until…

00:18:38.160 --> 00:18:42.560
Another big chunk that is only in the theatrical&nbsp;
cut. This one lasts 22 seconds and includes both&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:42.560 --> 00:18:45.680
of these shots. Then there’s one last&nbsp;
point where the two movies are in sync…

00:18:46.560 --> 00:18:49.600
And that is the last part we&nbsp;
see of Stab in Scre4m. There’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:49.600 --> 00:18:53.120
a little bit more Stab footage shown&nbsp;
in Scream 2 before the scene ends.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.120 --> 00:18:57.200
Well that was fun. Unless you skipped&nbsp;
it, in which case: moving right along.

00:18:57.200 --> 00:19:01.920
With the release of Gale Weathers’s new book in&nbsp;
1997, the race was on to make a sequel to Stab.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:01.920 --> 00:19:05.760
The producers of Stab would return, and&nbsp;
be joined by Floyd Malone and Fred Rifkin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:05.760 --> 00:19:09.360
who had worked with Milton before on Is&nbsp;
Somebody There? and 1st House on the Right.

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:12.560
This time, Jerry Rapp got the call&nbsp;
to direct. I’m not sure if this is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:12.560 --> 00:19:15.920
the same Jerry Rapp that went on&nbsp;
to write the movie: Looking Glass.

00:19:15.920 --> 00:19:19.840
Everyone else returned for Stab 2, but the&nbsp;
new character additions were decidedly less&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:24.640
star studded than the original film with Dusty&nbsp;
Penn as Sidney’s new boyfriend Derek Feldman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:24.640 --> 00:19:28.880
Sharon Batton as the sorority girl Cici&nbsp;
Cooper, KC Storm as the movie obsessed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.880 --> 00:19:32.960
killer Mickey Altieri, and Lauren Velez&nbsp;
as Sidney’s roommate Hallie McDaniel.

00:19:32.960 --> 00:19:37.200
Notably, there were two additional characters&nbsp;
added who weren’t a part of the real events:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:37.200 --> 00:19:40.240
Sonia and Alli. I’m guessing they were&nbsp;
brought on to be additional victims,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:40.240 --> 00:19:41.760
to add some extra gore to the movie.

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:45.280
Although the movie was produced and finished&nbsp;
very quickly, it was not without struggle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:45.280 --> 00:19:49.520
thanks to the high tensions between producers&nbsp;
John Milton and Fred Rifkin on set. According to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:49.520 --> 00:19:53.840
the Sunrise Sucks website, “the duo had a number&nbsp;
of run-ins over the years, but none matched the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:53.840 --> 00:19:58.640
one on (the) set of Stab 2, where there was a&nbsp;
huge blow-out on location at Columbia College,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:58.640 --> 00:20:02.080
a school known for producing the&nbsp;
iconic horror YouTuber: CZsWorld.

00:20:02.080 --> 00:20:06.080
Milton allegedly was going through drug addiction&nbsp;
issues at the time, and Rifken wanted him banned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.080 --> 00:20:10.000
from the set, but Tori Spelling used her&nbsp;
influence to protect Milton. As a result,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.000 --> 00:20:13.520
the production was split into two camps,&nbsp;
and things eventually boiled into a physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:13.520 --> 00:20:17.440
confrontation between Rifkin and Milton. The&nbsp;
two had to be pulled apart, and this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:17.440 --> 00:20:22.240
Milton was banned from being on set, which made&nbsp;
Spelling and other cast members very unhappy.

00:20:22.240 --> 00:20:26.400
Despite the drama, after much anticipation,&nbsp;
the Stab 2 trailer was released.

00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:29.040
– [female voice] Last night, two students were brutally murdered,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:29.040 --> 00:20:35.373
at a local movie theater.
(impact sound)

00:20:36.390 --> 00:20:40.634
(foreboding music)

00:20:40.634 --> 00:20:45.200
The first trailer for Stab 2 features a lot&nbsp;
of familiar scenes, yet, unfamiliar faces.

00:20:45.200 --> 00:20:47.520
– This is Gale Weathers reporting live.

00:20:47.520 --> 00:20:53.120
– [Zac] No. No it is not. This trailer was a bonus feature&nbsp;
on the DVD for Scream 3. The obvious answer as to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:53.120 --> 00:20:57.280
why the cast is filled with random people is&nbsp;
that like the other bonus stuff from Scream 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:57.280 --> 00:21:00.480
it’s not canon. But again, I’m gonna&nbsp;
come up with an excuse to incorporate&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:00.480 --> 00:21:03.280
it because it’s the closest thing&nbsp;
we have to footage from Stab 2.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.280 --> 00:21:08.080
Until a Scream movie gives us actual footage&nbsp;
of Stab 2, this is too much fun to leave out.

00:21:08.080 --> 00:21:12.240
So think about it like this. In Scream&nbsp;
2, the movie within the movie is Stab.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.240 --> 00:21:16.160
But if Stab 2 is the in-universe equivalent&nbsp;
of Scream 2, then there has to be another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.160 --> 00:21:20.400
movie within the movie in Stab 2. The movie&nbsp;
that the Stab versions of Maureen Evans and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:20.400 --> 00:21:23.840
Phil Stevens go to see at the beginning.&nbsp;
I think that’s what we’re seeing here.

00:21:23.840 --> 00:21:27.680
Just as this clip was originally used to&nbsp;
promote Scream 3 in our real life universe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:31.440
it would also have been used in the Scream&nbsp;
universe to promote Stab 2. There is actually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:31.440 --> 00:21:35.840
some precedent for the Stab movies inside the&nbsp;
Stab franchise still just being called Stab.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:35.840 --> 00:21:41.040
Remember that the opening of Stab 7 is two girls&nbsp;
sitting on a couch watching a movie called Stab 6.

00:21:41.040 --> 00:21:44.400
One interesting thing about the Stab 2&nbsp;
trailer is that it seems to stray further&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:44.400 --> 00:21:48.720
from the actual events of Scream 2 than the&nbsp;
original Stab did. For one, Windsor College is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:53.600
now “Columbia University.” There are also brand&nbsp;
new Ghostface scenes, seemingly new characters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:53.600 --> 00:21:57.440
I’m guessing this is Alli, played by Angela&nbsp;
Kong, and even some creative liberties in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:57.440 --> 00:22:01.280
the dialogue. For example, in Scream 2,&nbsp;
we never hear the end of the third rule.

00:22:01.280 --> 00:22:05.360
– [Randy] And number three, if you want your&nbsp;
sequel to become a franchise, never ever--&nbsp;

00:22:05.360 --> 00:22:07.840
– How do we find the killer&nbsp;
Randy? That’s what I want to know.

00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:10.400
– But in the Stab 2 trailer,&nbsp;
we hear all of them in full.

00:22:10.400 --> 00:22:15.440
– Number one, body count’s always higher.&nbsp;
Number two, the death scenes are much more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.440 --> 00:22:20.720
elaborate. Number three, never ever under any&nbsp;
circumstances assume that the killer is dead.

00:22:20.720 --> 00:22:24.800
– The hype must have been high for Stab 2, because&nbsp;
it seems that before the movie was even released,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:24.800 --> 00:22:29.760
the studio greenlit another sequel. Stab 2 was&nbsp;
most likely released in the summer of 1999,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:29.760 --> 00:22:33.360
based on the fact that while Stab&nbsp;
3 was shooting in August 1999,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:33.360 --> 00:22:36.240
they are actively advertising&nbsp;
Stab 2 on buses in LA.

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:40.320
The movie begins with two new murders at a&nbsp;
movie sneak preview of Stab. It’s discovered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:40.320 --> 00:22:44.720
both are students at Sidney Prescott and Randy&nbsp;
Meeks’s college. Naturally, Gale Weathers shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:44.720 --> 00:22:49.200
up to cover the story, but this time has to go up&nbsp;
against another reporter who goes by Deborah Salt.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:49.200 --> 00:22:53.520
Gale attempts to set up a reconciliation interview&nbsp;
between Sidney and Cotton, but Sidney is not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:53.520 --> 00:22:57.760
interested. Dewey also flies into town to try to&nbsp;
help protect Sid when he hears what’s going on.

00:22:57.760 --> 00:23:00.720
That night, a sorority girl named&nbsp;
Cici gets taken out by Ghostface,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:00.720 --> 00:23:05.200
and Sidney narrowly avoids the knife herself. Her&nbsp;
boyfriend, Derek, helps fight off the intruder,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:05.200 --> 00:23:09.600
but since nobody actually saw his fight with&nbsp;
Ghostface, he becomes a suspect, due to the injury&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:09.600 --> 00:23:14.080
on his arm conveniently missing any critical&nbsp;
arteries, and he is a medical student after all.

00:23:14.080 --> 00:23:18.480
Sidney’s roommate, Hallie, and her film nerd&nbsp;
friend Mickey also get interrogated and Sidney&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:18.480 --> 00:23:22.160
is given a personal security detail. The&nbsp;
next day, Derek tries to calm Sidney’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:22.160 --> 00:23:25.920
worries by serenading her in front of the whole&nbsp;
dining hall and giving her his Greek letters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.920 --> 00:23:29.760
but a Ghostface encounter during a rehearsal for&nbsp;
a play that she’s in still leaves her shaken.

00:23:29.760 --> 00:23:33.760
Dewey, Gale and Randy determine that the culprit&nbsp;
is trying to recreate what happened in Woodsboro,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:33.760 --> 00:23:37.440
but this time using the rules and tropes that&nbsp;
dictate horror movie sequels. They receive a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:37.440 --> 00:23:42.160
mysterious call, but while trying to track down&nbsp;
the caller, Randy is abducted and loses his life.

00:23:42.160 --> 00:23:46.080
Meanwhile, Sidney has a scary confrontation with&nbsp;
Cotton Weary, who still wants his interview,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:46.080 --> 00:23:49.520
and he’s arrested, if only temporarily,&nbsp;
as they have no evidence against him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.520 --> 00:23:53.360
Not long after, Dewey and Gale are attacked&nbsp;
while reviewing film evidence and Sidney and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:53.360 --> 00:23:56.960
Hallie are attacked while fleeing town in&nbsp;
a vehicle with Sidney’s security guards.

00:23:56.960 --> 00:23:59.920
Sidney ends up being the only one to&nbsp;
get away from the car crash alive and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:59.920 --> 00:24:04.000
soon after finds herself lured into a trap on&nbsp;
the stage of the play she was set to star in.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:04.000 --> 00:24:06.080
After sacrificing Sidney’s&nbsp;
boyfriend in front of her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:06.080 --> 00:24:09.760
the killers reveal themselves as Mickey&nbsp;
and Deborah Salt, or as Sidney knows her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:09.760 --> 00:24:13.920
Mrs. Loomis, Billy’s vengeful mother. With a&nbsp;
little help from Gale and eventually Cotton,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:13.920 --> 00:24:18.240
she’s able to defeat her attackers once again&nbsp;
and Dewey even lives to see another day.

00:24:18.240 --> 00:24:22.480
As I mentioned, production on Stab 3: Return to&nbsp;
Woodsboro had already begun at some point during&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:22.480 --> 00:24:27.040
the second movie’s theatrical run. Location&nbsp;
scouting began in June 1999, and casting was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:27.040 --> 00:24:31.040
completed before the end of the first week of&nbsp;
June. This time, much of the original cast did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:31.040 --> 00:24:36.080
not come back, including Tori Spelling, David&nbsp;
Schwimmer and Craig Bierko. Some have speculated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:39.840
that they did not feel comfortable starring in&nbsp;
a franchise that was connected to so much real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:39.840 --> 00:24:43.680
crime, but I would guess that maybe they weren’t&nbsp;
interested in doing a fictional continuation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:43.680 --> 00:24:48.720
of a nonfiction story. Kind of like how Kate&nbsp;
Winslet wasn’t in Titanic II. It’s also likely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:48.720 --> 00:24:53.200
that Tori Spelling did not want to return thanks&nbsp;
to infighting and tension on set during Stab 2.

00:24:53.200 --> 00:24:57.600
The studio held a massive talent search to find&nbsp;
the next Sidney Prescott, in which 50,000 girls&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:24:57.600 --> 00:25:01.760
tried out. The part ended up going to newcomer&nbsp;
Angelina Tyler, which she was able to secure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:01.760 --> 00:25:06.320
by sleeping with the producer, John Milton. The&nbsp;
new Deputy Dewey Riley would be actor Tom Prinze,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:06.320 --> 00:25:09.440
who was well enough to play the part&nbsp;
despite crashing his car a month prior.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:09.440 --> 00:25:13.120
To fill Craig Bierko’s role as Cotton Weary,&nbsp;
they simply got the real Cotton Weary to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:13.120 --> 00:25:17.680
make a small cameo as himself. Jennifer&nbsp;
Jolie reprised her role as Gale Weathers.

00:25:17.680 --> 00:25:21.040
Since many fans were upset about the&nbsp;
decision to kill off Randy in Stab 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:21.040 --> 00:25:25.040
a new character named Ricky, another video&nbsp;
store employee, was created as an homage&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:25.040 --> 00:25:29.120
and the role was given to Tyson Fox. Fox is&nbsp;
rumored to be the illegitimate son of Sunrise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:29.120 --> 00:25:34.000
Studios head Don Crosby and actress Leticia&nbsp;
Yanci. Similarly, the new character, Candy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:34.000 --> 00:25:38.560
seems to be an homage to Tatum, from Stab 1,&nbsp;
and this spot would be filled by 35-year-old&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:38.560 --> 00:25:42.320
Sarah Darling, at the request of her agent…&nbsp;
even though she wasn’t thrilled about it.

00:25:42.320 --> 00:25:46.240
– Stab 3. Jesus I gotta get a new agent.

00:25:46.240 --> 00:25:49.200
– Supporting cast included Dan&nbsp;
Arrdondo as the Swimming Coach,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:49.200 --> 00:25:54.240
Barry Barclay as Computer Geek #1 and Brett&nbsp;
Riley as Obsessive Fan. It seems the movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:54.240 --> 00:25:58.720
also included multiple Ghostface killers, a&nbsp;
sheriff, a principal, an innocent crash victim,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:25:58.720 --> 00:26:03.360
a hooker, an english teacher, a woman named&nbsp;
Jan and a man named Ted, among others.

00:26:03.360 --> 00:26:07.280
As for the production team, Fred Rifkin was&nbsp;
initially supposed to direct, but according to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:07.280 --> 00:26:11.680
documents in Sunrise Sucks, he was already not in&nbsp;
good standing with other producers after repeated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:11.680 --> 00:26:16.400
conflicts while shooting Stab 2. However, he&nbsp;
wasn’t removed until after one scandalous evening,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:16.400 --> 00:26:21.840
where John Milton shared a $25,000 hotel room with&nbsp;
Hollywood agent Beverly Choi. Choi represents the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:21.840 --> 00:26:26.160
acclaimed music video director Roman Bridger,&nbsp;
who happens to be Maureen Prescott’s bastard son.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:26.160 --> 00:26:30.240
Just two days after Choi and Milton slept&nbsp;
together, Rifkin was fired from Stab 3 and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:30.240 --> 00:26:34.320
Roman was brought on to replace him as part&nbsp;
of a two movie deal with Sunrise Studios.

00:26:34.320 --> 00:26:38.000
Strangely, in this interview with Gale&nbsp;
Weathers, Roman claims that he cannot reveal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:38.000 --> 00:26:42.080
anything about Stab 3, including basic&nbsp;
information about who’s starring in it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:42.080 --> 00:26:46.320
due to a sworn secrecy with Milton, who he&nbsp;
refers to as “Master Milton.” Apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:46.320 --> 00:26:50.720
this confidentiality is so secretive that he can’t&nbsp;
even communicate with Jerry Rapp, the director of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:50.720 --> 00:26:55.600
Stab 2, about it. This extreme dedication to&nbsp;
secrecy extended to Will Kennison’s script.

00:26:55.600 --> 00:26:58.400
– The producers told us that there were&nbsp;
three different versions of the script,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:26:58.400 --> 00:27:00.080
something about trying to keep&nbsp;
the ending off the internet.

00:27:00.080 --> 00:27:04.080
– Another challenge arose when longtime Director&nbsp;
of Photography Hawken Jones passed away&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:04.080 --> 00:27:06.640
not long before shooting, and&nbsp;
was replaced by Peter Deming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:06.640 --> 00:27:10.320
who shot Evil Dead 2 and Austin&nbsp;
Powers: International Man of Mystery.

00:27:10.320 --> 00:27:14.000
The only other notable crew brought on&nbsp;
was Dewey Riley himself, who was hired as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:14.000 --> 00:27:18.080
a technical advisor due to his experience&nbsp;
in surviving both Woodsboro and Windsor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:18.080 --> 00:27:21.760
Jennifer Jolie credited Dewey with helping&nbsp;
her understand the real Gale Weathers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:21.760 --> 00:27:26.240
but it’s likely that Dewey only took the position&nbsp;
as a crutch after his break up with the real Gale.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:26.240 --> 00:27:30.560
If he couldn’t have the woman he wanted, being&nbsp;
around her best imitator was the next best thing.

00:27:30.560 --> 00:27:31.920
In addition to all that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:31.920 --> 00:27:35.840
John Milton allegedly still owed Gale&nbsp;
money for the right to adapt her books.

00:27:35.840 --> 00:27:39.120
Despite all the tension and drama&nbsp;
during the pre-production stages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:39.120 --> 00:27:42.800
Stab 3’s production would make those&nbsp;
problems look like a walk in the park,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:42.800 --> 00:27:47.036
as things were about to get&nbsp;
a whole lot more serious.

00:27:47.036 --> 00:27:50.483
(impact)

00:27:51.780 --> 00:27:55.270
(dark music)

00:27:55.520 --> 00:27:58.560
Distribution and production meetings&nbsp;
for Stab 3: Return to Woodsboro were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:27:58.560 --> 00:28:02.000
held near the end of June, with principal&nbsp;
photography most likely beginning in July,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:02.000 --> 00:28:04.800
with plans to continue shooting&nbsp;
through August and into September,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:04.800 --> 00:28:07.280
wrapping with second unit shoots&nbsp;
in the latter half of the month.

00:28:07.280 --> 00:28:09.680
Though we don’t know much about&nbsp;
the story, due to its fictional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:09.680 --> 00:28:13.280
nature and the fact that it was never&nbsp;
completed, we can pick up little details.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:13.280 --> 00:28:17.120
August 2 and August 3 are shot in a&nbsp;
location called “Dorm Bathroom” and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:17.120 --> 00:28:20.400
the first kill of the movie involves the&nbsp;
Candy getting killed while in the shower.

00:28:20.400 --> 00:28:24.160
Other shoot locations include Sid’s&nbsp;
Dorm Room, the Library , a Coffee House,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:24.160 --> 00:28:28.240
and Sid’s party. This would suggest that Sidney&nbsp;
is still in college in this movie. The others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:28.240 --> 00:28:32.720
are fairly generic locations: houses, a&nbsp;
hospital, and somewhere called Grant Hill.

00:28:32.720 --> 00:28:35.360
Obviously the subtitle of the&nbsp;
film is “Return to Woodsboro”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:35.360 --> 00:28:40.000
so a big part of it must be the main location:&nbsp;
Woodsboro. We see huge sets being constructed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:40.000 --> 00:28:43.360
including Sidney’s house, Stu&nbsp;
Macher’s house, the TV news van,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:43.360 --> 00:28:47.280
Sidney’s childhood room, and even Stu’s&nbsp;
garage, which judging by the open fridge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:47.280 --> 00:28:50.720
and prop blood on the pet flap, looks&nbsp;
to be a part of a series of flashbacks.

00:28:50.720 --> 00:28:54.560
Based on this, I’m going to infer that Stab&nbsp;
3: Return to Woodsboro was about a new pair of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:54.560 --> 00:28:58.800
Ghostface killers who start attacking more people&nbsp;
at Sidney’s college, prompting administrators&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:28:58.800 --> 00:29:02.560
to cancel classes and send students home --&nbsp;
essentially the college version of what happened&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:02.560 --> 00:29:07.120
in Stab 1 and Scream 1. Returning home causes&nbsp;
Sidney to have a flashback to her mother’s murder.

00:29:07.120 --> 00:29:11.360
– [Tom] Scene 34. Maureen’s murder flashback.

00:29:11.360 --> 00:29:14.560
– [Zac] Once she’s back in Woodsboro, the killers&nbsp;
try to recreate each of the scenes from the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:14.560 --> 00:29:17.840
original Stab, with the new addition of&nbsp;
Ricky, who gets a huge pair of scissors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:17.840 --> 00:29:20.880
through the head. But this time, the&nbsp;
killers hope to avenge Billy and Stu&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:20.880 --> 00:29:25.040
by defeating Sidney at the end of the movie.&nbsp;
As Sidney revisits the locations of her past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:25.040 --> 00:29:29.200
she’s also forced to relive flashbacks to&nbsp;
the horrors that she experienced around town.

00:29:29.200 --> 00:29:34.400
– [Tom] Scene 40 though 47. The&nbsp;
Prescott house flashback.

00:29:34.400 --> 00:29:38.658
– [Zac] But of course she’s not alone, she has new&nbsp;
and old friends that are looking out for her.

00:29:38.658 --> 00:29:43.241
– [Tom] Scene 50. Dewey saves the day.

00:29:43.360 --> 00:29:47.360
– This is all kind of ironic though, because&nbsp;
Roman is the director of Return to Woodsboro&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:47.360 --> 00:29:51.760
and in real life, he is also recreating many&nbsp;
of the kills, using the cast of his movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:51.760 --> 00:29:56.560
as his victims. He’s also probably recruited&nbsp;
Angelina Tyler, aka: the new Sidney actress,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:29:56.560 --> 00:30:00.640
to assist him as the second killer. If you want to&nbsp;
know more about that, watch my Scream 3 Things You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:00.640 --> 00:30:04.320
Missed, where I talk about the fan theory that&nbsp;
Angelina is the second killer in that movie.

00:30:04.320 --> 00:30:07.840
Shooting has begun, and it’s late July&nbsp;
or early August, based on the fact that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:07.840 --> 00:30:11.600
Candy is preparing to shoot her death scene,&nbsp;
which is going to roll on August 2 and 3,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:11.600 --> 00:30:14.800
when Roman takes his first victim:&nbsp;
Cotton Weary. When the news breaks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:14.800 --> 00:30:18.720
the producers and studio executives get&nbsp;
together to discuss possibly pulling the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:18.720 --> 00:30:22.160
plug on the movie, but of course, since&nbsp;
there’s money to be made, they don’t.

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:25.920
– Detectives! There’s no reason to presume&nbsp;
that Cotton’s death had anything to do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:25.920 --> 00:30:28.320
this movie, is there?
– He was making a movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:28.320 --> 00:30:31.200
called Stab. He was stabbed.

00:30:31.200 --> 00:30:34.320
– Candy is the next target. Roman calls&nbsp;
her into an after hours meeting in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:34.320 --> 00:30:37.200
the production office and one of the&nbsp;
killers corners her in the prop room.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:37.200 --> 00:30:40.720
The entire staff is interrogated by&nbsp;
police, and the movie is shut down.

00:30:40.720 --> 00:30:44.960
But that doesn’t stop the body count from&nbsp;
rising. First, Jennifer Jolie’s bodyguard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:44.960 --> 00:30:49.760
Steven Stone gets the knife. The rest of the cast&nbsp;
receives faxes with new script pages. So Roman is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:49.760 --> 00:30:54.320
essentially rewriting his movie in real time, and&nbsp;
the next scene involves Tom Prinze accidentally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:30:54.320 --> 00:30:58.640
using a lighter in Jennifer’s gas-filled&nbsp;
house, causing it to explode with him inside.

00:30:58.640 --> 00:31:02.800
Roman also tracks down Sidney, who is living&nbsp;
a secret life of seclusion up to that point,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:02.800 --> 00:31:07.440
so she travels to LA to be with Dewey and Gale&nbsp;
so that she doesn’t feel alone. Roman almost seems&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:07.440 --> 00:31:11.760
to be recreating the events of the Stab&nbsp;
3: Return to Woodsboro script, only instead of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:11.760 --> 00:31:15.840
Sidney returning to the real Woodsboro, she’s&nbsp;
returning to the Woodsboro set in Hollywood.

00:31:15.840 --> 00:31:19.360
The gang also receives a tape from Randy Meeks,&nbsp;
who explains that they are once again dealing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:19.360 --> 00:31:23.040
with a new set of rules; this time they are&nbsp;
in the midst of a trilogy final chapter.

00:31:23.040 --> 00:31:25.440
While exploring the movie set&nbsp;
version of her old neighborhood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:25.440 --> 00:31:29.680
Sidney is nearly killed by the Ghostface duo, so&nbsp;
she’s taken to the police station for protection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:29.680 --> 00:31:33.360
While this is going on, Gale, Jennifer&nbsp;
and Dewey show up in John Milton’s office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:33.360 --> 00:31:37.280
to confront him about their discovery that&nbsp;
Maureen Prescott was in his movies in the 70s.

00:31:37.280 --> 00:31:40.800
Afterwards, they receive a call from a voice&nbsp;
who they believe to be Sidney telling them&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:40.800 --> 00:31:44.240
she’ll be going to Roman’s birthday party&nbsp;
at John Milton’s mansion, so they decide&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:44.240 --> 00:31:48.400
to meet her there. Unfortunately for them,&nbsp;
it’s a trap. They discover a voice changer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:48.400 --> 00:31:52.320
cell phone, and Ghostface costume in a&nbsp;
closet, and Gale comes across Roman’s body,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:52.320 --> 00:31:55.760
and incorrectly believes that he’s dead,&nbsp;
making it easier for him to take out his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:31:55.760 --> 00:31:59.520
partner Angelina, and then Tyson and&nbsp;
Jennifer without drawing suspicion.

00:31:59.520 --> 00:32:04.320
Using Dewey and Gale as hostages, he calls Sidney&nbsp;
over to the party, for real this time. He reveals&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:04.320 --> 00:32:08.800
himself in the secret screening room, and explains&nbsp;
his entire motive, his desire to get revenge on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:08.800 --> 00:32:12.400
the family that shunned him away. He brings&nbsp;
out the hostage that he’s planning to frame:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:12.400 --> 00:32:16.320
John Milton, who Roman sees as the source&nbsp;
of his issues, because he allowed Maureen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:16.320 --> 00:32:21.040
to be taken advantage of by Hollywood predators,&nbsp;
causing him to be born in shame, and abandoned.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:21.040 --> 00:32:25.200
Sidney catches him off guard, and with Dewey’s&nbsp;
help, she’s able to eventually finish him off.

00:32:25.200 --> 00:32:28.080
Sidney returns home with her friends,&nbsp;
no longer having to worry about her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:28.080 --> 00:32:31.840
past coming forward to haunt her, and&nbsp;
Dewey proposes to Gale -- she accepts.

00:32:31.840 --> 00:32:35.840
If all of that sounds like the plot of a&nbsp;
movie, Sunrise Studios agrees with you,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:35.840 --> 00:32:40.000
because in place of Stab 3: Return to Woodsboro,&nbsp;
they decided to go forward with a new movie:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:40.000 --> 00:32:44.480
Stab 3: Hollywood Horror, based on the failed&nbsp;
production of Stab 3: Return to Woodsboro.

00:32:44.480 --> 00:32:48.720
As usual, first came the book, Hollywood Horror&nbsp;
by Gale Weathers. We don’t know exactly when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:48.720 --> 00:32:52.400
movie came out, but there’s a good chance they&nbsp;
waited a little while. For one, they needed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:52.400 --> 00:32:56.640
wait for Gale’s book to come out and reveal the&nbsp;
ending. They also needed to find a new producer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:32:56.640 --> 00:33:00.800
and an entirely new cast, unless of course Tori&nbsp;
Spelling was willing to come back now that it was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:00.800 --> 00:33:05.280
based on a true story again. Perhaps she would do&nbsp;
the movie in honor of her good friend John Milton?

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:08.480
It also makes sense that they’d take&nbsp;
a break before doing the next movie.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:08.480 --> 00:33:12.080
Not that they’ve ever cared about capitalizing&nbsp;
too soon on a tragedy, but they’d probably&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:12.080 --> 00:33:15.760
want to take a step back and up their&nbsp;
security before beginning another project.

00:33:15.760 --> 00:33:19.520
Regardless of when it came out, it seems like&nbsp;
there is a significant time gap in the narrative.

00:33:19.520 --> 00:33:22.400
– How long have you and Dewey been married?
– Ten years.&nbsp;

00:33:22.400 --> 00:33:25.440
– Aww, just like your characters in Stab 3. Wow!

00:33:25.440 --> 00:33:28.800
– I’m not sure if they were married at the end&nbsp;
of Stab 2, or if this is just something that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:28.800 --> 00:33:32.720
happened in between movies, but it seems&nbsp;
to suggest that Stab 3: Hollywood Horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:32.720 --> 00:33:37.520
is set in 2007 or later. However, it must have&nbsp;
come out before that and been set in the near&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:41.120
future -- assuming they aren’t releasing&nbsp;
more than one Stab per year, that is.

00:33:41.120 --> 00:33:44.080
We can assume that it did pretty well&nbsp;
at the box office, because the franchise&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:44.080 --> 00:33:47.600
would soon be brought back with another&nbsp;
attempt at a fictional revival. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:47.600 --> 00:33:50.400
the real Sidney Prescott did&nbsp;
not like her story being used,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:50.400 --> 00:33:53.760
and it’s easy to see why considering all&nbsp;
the trouble the movies have caused her.

00:33:53.760 --> 00:33:56.480
– [Jenny] But then she threatened to&nbsp;
sue them if they used her story,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:33:56.480 --> 00:33:58.160
so then they just started making stuff up.

00:33:58.160 --> 00:34:01.440
–  [Zac] So the Sidney character that they had&nbsp;
created continued to be in the movies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:01.440 --> 00:34:05.040
but they were no longer related to any&nbsp;
events relating to her real life.

00:34:05.040 --> 00:34:07.200
Gale Weathers also wanted to continue cashing in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:07.200 --> 00:34:10.960
on the Ghostface IP with a fictional&nbsp;
installment, so she wrote this book,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:10.960 --> 00:34:15.280
Ghostface Returns, though it’s not clear if&nbsp;
this was before or after the release of Stab&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:15.280 --> 00:34:19.360
4. I’d assume one of the post-trilogy films&nbsp;
was probably based off of this book though.

00:34:19.360 --> 00:34:23.440
Next up was Stab 5: Clock of Doom, which&nbsp;
introduced time travel into the fray,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:23.440 --> 00:34:25.760
which did not go over well with fans.

00:34:25.760 --> 00:34:28.160
– [Richie] This franchise goes off&nbsp;
the rails with number five.&nbsp;

00:34:28.160 --> 00:34:31.840
– [Jenny] Stab 5 has time travel,&nbsp;
which is by far the worst.

00:34:31.840 --> 00:34:36.720
–  Still, the movie did well enough to justify a&nbsp;
sixth film. Stab 6 brings the franchise into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:36.720 --> 00:34:41.360
the era of social media by introducing a duo of&nbsp;
Facebook killers. Not much is known about the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:41.360 --> 00:34:46.160
actual movie, Stab 6, that exists in the Scream&nbsp;
universe. Remember, I’m going off the assumption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:46.160 --> 00:34:50.880
that the Stab movies contain a fictional franchise&nbsp;
of movies-within-the-movie, also called Stab.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:50.880 --> 00:34:54.720
Going off the logic established in Scream 2,&nbsp;
where the actors in Stab are different people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:54.720 --> 00:34:58.960
than the actors in Scream 1, we can assume that&nbsp;
the footage on your screen right now is not an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:34:58.960 --> 00:35:04.240
actual clip of Stab 6, but rather, the movie&nbsp;
within the movie in Stab 7. But like Stab 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:04.240 --> 00:35:08.320
it’s probably very close to the real thing with&nbsp;
only a few minor differences, so let’s explore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:08.320 --> 00:35:13.751
the opening of Stab 6, which contains one detail&nbsp;
that tells us more than you might have realized.

00:35:13.751 --> 00:35:16.886
(impact)

00:35:18.691 --> 00:35:21.558
(phone ringing)

00:35:21.558 --> 00:35:23.719
– [Sherry] Hello?
– [caller] Hello.&nbsp;

00:35:23.719 --> 00:35:26.800
– [Sherry] Uhh yes?
– [caller] Who is this?

00:35:26.800 --> 00:35:30.560
– Stab 6 opens up with an homage to the&nbsp;
original Stab, a teenage girl answering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:30.560 --> 00:35:34.160
a phone call from a mysterious caller.&nbsp;
Our characters are Sherry and Trudy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:34.160 --> 00:35:37.840
and like Casey from that original movie, they&nbsp;
are getting ready to watch a horror movie.

00:35:37.840 --> 00:35:42.160
Sherry picks Saw IV, and although Trudy isn’t&nbsp;
a fan, this helps us place the movie in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:42.160 --> 00:35:47.200
Stab timeline, because Saw IV was released on&nbsp;
DVD and Blu-ray in January 2008, meaning that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:47.200 --> 00:35:51.680
this movie takes place some time after that.&nbsp;
We already established that Stab 4 takes place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:51.680 --> 00:35:57.200
at the very earliest, in 2007, and Stab 5 has&nbsp;
time travel, making it impossible to place,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:35:57.200 --> 00:36:01.120
so ‘08 seems reasonable here, especially&nbsp;
given that it involves a Facebook killer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:01.120 --> 00:36:05.120
Trudy is using a Sidekick phone and&nbsp;
Sherry still has standard definition TV.

00:36:05.120 --> 00:36:08.000
The two discuss Trudy’s Facebook&nbsp;
stalker when the phone rings with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:08.000 --> 00:36:09.703
a threatening voice on the other line.

00:36:09.703 --> 00:36:13.040
– YOU HANG UP ON ME AND I’LL CUT&nbsp;
THROUGH YOUR NECK UNTIL I FEEL BONE!

00:36:13.040 --> 00:36:16.160
– [Zac] Trudy suddenly becomes worried, and checks&nbsp;
to make sure the doors are locked when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:16.160 --> 00:36:20.080
doorbell rings and a message comes in&nbsp;
daring them to open it. Sherry does so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:20.080 --> 00:36:24.800
to reveal seemingly no one, but another text&nbsp;
gives them a better idea of the killer’s location.

00:36:24.800 --> 00:36:27.280
– It says, ‘I’m not outside. I’m right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:27.280 --> 00:36:28.920
beside you.’
(screams)

00:36:29.520 --> 00:36:32.240
– [Zac] And when Sherry tries to run,&nbsp;
she’s cut off by the second killer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:32.240 --> 00:36:34.240
who makes good on the promise about her neck.

00:36:34.240 --> 00:36:39.920
And that is basically, all I can tell you about&nbsp;
Stab 6, if indeed that actually happens in Stab 6,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:39.920 --> 00:36:43.440
because the clips you just saw are&nbsp;
from Stab 7. So let’s move onto Stab 7.

00:36:43.440 --> 00:36:47.760
For the first time in a while, I can definitively&nbsp;
tell you a little bit about the cast. Stab 6&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:47.760 --> 00:36:51.760
Sherry was played by Lucy Hale. We can’t say for&nbsp;
sure what she might have been known for at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:51.760 --> 00:36:57.200
time, because we don’t know exactly when Stab 7,&nbsp;
or any of the other post-trilogy Stabs came out.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:36:57.200 --> 00:37:02.000
My best guess is that it came out in 2008, because&nbsp;
in Scream 4, Charlie says that Stab-a-thon is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:02.000 --> 00:37:06.480
a marathon of all 7 Stab movies and that this&nbsp;
is their 3rd annual Stab-a-thon. However, I’m&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:06.480 --> 00:37:10.800
not gonna nail that down, because it’s entirely&nbsp;
possible that in previous years, Stab-a-thon was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:10.800 --> 00:37:15.360
only a 6 movie marathon. So it’s impossible&nbsp;
to say when movies 4-7 came out for sure.

00:37:15.360 --> 00:37:18.880
We can’t even really use that Saw 4&nbsp;
reference in Stab 6 to help nail down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:18.880 --> 00:37:22.160
a date, because the later Stab films&nbsp;
all take place in the near future,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:22.160 --> 00:37:25.760
so the existence of a fourth Saw movie&nbsp;
could have very well just been a prediction.

00:37:25.760 --> 00:37:30.560
Anyway, Lucy Hale is joined by Shenae Grimes as&nbsp;
her friend, Trudie. The actual Stab 7 characters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:30.560 --> 00:37:35.040
are Chloe and Rachel. Chloe is played by Kristen&nbsp;
Bell, and Rachel is played by Anna Paquin,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:35.040 --> 00:37:39.760
who was a child star who started acting at four&nbsp;
years old and played Rogue in the 2000 X-men.

00:37:39.760 --> 00:37:43.600
After watching the intro of Stab 6, Rachel&nbsp;
turns off the movie, and complains that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:43.600 --> 00:37:47.920
whole self-aware post-modern meta horror&nbsp;
has been done to death. Chloe argues that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:47.920 --> 00:37:52.080
she likes the realism of the Stab… which is&nbsp;
ironic considering Stab 5 had time travel,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:37:52.080 --> 00:37:56.720
but Rachel counters by ragging on the formulaic&nbsp;
nature of a franchise with too many sequels.

00:37:56.720 --> 00:38:00.080
– [Rachel] There’s no element of&nbsp;
surprise, you can see everything&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:00.080 --> 00:38:01.920
coming.
(scream)

00:38:01.920 --> 00:38:05.440
– [Zac] Chloe tells her to shut up and watch the&nbsp;
movie, and with one more stab, she turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:05.440 --> 00:38:10.000
the movie back on and continues watching. It&nbsp;
seems that Stab 7 shares the goals of Scream 4&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:10.000 --> 00:38:14.480
in that it’s all about doing the unexpected. Not&nbsp;
only is the timing of Chloe’s attack surprising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:19.040
but the intro also leaves us with a surviving&nbsp;
killer who we know the identity of for the first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:19.040 --> 00:38:24.160
time. In this sense, Stab 7 directly addresses the&nbsp;
fans’ criticism about the movies’ predictability.

00:38:24.160 --> 00:38:28.640
Unfortunately for Stab fans, the series seems&nbsp;
to have a hiatus after this, another example&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:28.640 --> 00:38:33.520
of it running parallel to the Scream franchise.&nbsp;
At some point, Sunrise released a Stab box set,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:33.520 --> 00:38:37.760
which included a documentary about the true story&nbsp;
behind the Stab trilogy. The box set contains&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:37.760 --> 00:38:42.720
Stabs 1-7 on Blu-ray. The films would also make&nbsp;
their way onto the streaming website Netflix.

00:38:42.720 --> 00:38:46.960
After over a decade of waiting, fans would&nbsp;
be subjected to a new self-titled movie:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:46.960 --> 00:38:51.920
Stab (2021). It seems the new iteration was&nbsp;
directed or produced by Rian Johnson, who had made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:51.920 --> 00:38:56.640
quite a name for himself over the previous&nbsp;
decade with Looper, The Last Jedi and Knives Out.

00:38:56.640 --> 00:38:59.360
To promote the movie, Johnson&nbsp;
did an interview on Dead Meat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:38:59.360 --> 00:39:03.760
The film clocked in at a whopping 2 hours&nbsp;
and 32 minutes and was given an R rating.

00:39:03.760 --> 00:39:07.680
Stab (2021) appears to be another fictional&nbsp;
endeavor, despite the fact that the real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:07.680 --> 00:39:11.520
Sidney did survive a a new set of killers in&nbsp;
2010 in Woodsboro, where she was attacked by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:11.520 --> 00:39:15.200
her cousin and her boyfriend. It’s likely&nbsp;
that Sunrise Studios did not try to use&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:15.200 --> 00:39:19.200
this story because of Sidney’s threats of a&nbsp;
lawsuit if they used her real story. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:19.200 --> 00:39:22.720
this would be the first Stab film not to&nbsp;
feature a Sidney Prescott character at all.

00:39:22.720 --> 00:39:25.280
– [caller] Sidney’s in every movie but the last one.

00:39:25.280 --> 00:39:27.760
– But even though this eighth&nbsp;
installment would mostly be fictional,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:27.760 --> 00:39:30.320
there are little connections to&nbsp;
the second Woodsboro murders,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:30.320 --> 00:39:33.440
like the inclusion of a character&nbsp;
to represent Deputy Judy Hicks.

00:39:33.440 --> 00:39:35.040
– Jesus, my mom was a character in one of them…

00:39:35.040 --> 00:39:39.360
– …and the title, simply being: Stab, which&nbsp;
suggests this movie is probably a requel.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:39.360 --> 00:39:44.000
The killers in the second Woodsboro murders&nbsp;
(aka: Scream 4) were out to create a requel.

00:39:44.000 --> 00:39:46.160
Only one casting choice is known. Matthew&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:39:46.160 --> 00:39:49.360
Lillard was the voice of the new&nbsp;
villain: Flamethrower Ghostface.

00:39:49.360 --> 00:39:54.800
Despite the copious amounts of hype for this&nbsp;
movie, fans seemed to really dislike it.

00:39:54.800 --> 00:40:00.320
– [man computer] And don’t even get me started on&nbsp;
Ghostface’s signature new weapon.&nbsp;

00:40:00.320 --> 00:40:03.600
– [Ghostface] That sh*t is lit.
– [woman on computer] And can we talk about the title,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:03.600 --> 00:40:08.880
Stab? What, just like the original? Just call it Stab 8. It’s f*cking Stab 8,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:08.880 --> 00:40:09.920
you’re not fooling anyone.

00:40:10.800 --> 00:40:15.040
– [Zac] Between Ghostface’s new silver face, the&nbsp;
replacement of his robe with a sleeveless hoodie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:15.040 --> 00:40:19.200
the use of a flamethrower, which is a weapon&nbsp;
that does not even Stab it’s victims, the fact&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:19.200 --> 00:40:23.680
that the killer is so clearly male, eliminating&nbsp;
probably 50% of the suspects and thus making the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:23.680 --> 00:40:27.760
movie far more predictable, the lack of legacy&nbsp;
characters and franchise connections, and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:27.760 --> 00:40:32.320
departure from numeric naming conventions, people&nbsp;
were already calling for the studio to redo it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:32.320 --> 00:40:38.400
using the hashtags #RedoStab8 and&nbsp;
#RedoStab2021 to drum up a movement.

00:40:38.400 --> 00:40:42.960
– You go on 4chan and Dreadit, all they’re talking&nbsp;
about is how Stab 8 pissed on their childhoods.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:42.960 --> 00:40:46.720
How they crammed in social commentary just to make&nbsp;
it elevated. How the main character’s a Mary Sue.&nbsp;

00:40:46.720 --> 00:40:48.720
– What’s a Mary Sue?
– You really don’t want to know.

00:40:48.720 --> 00:40:52.000
– The film was so bad, it even&nbsp;
drove a crazed superfan to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:40:52.000 --> 00:40:54.320
engage in a new set of Woodsboro murders.

00:40:54.320 --> 00:40:59.120
– Anyway, it sucked balls. Because nobody&nbsp;
takes the true fans seriously. Not really.

00:40:59.120 --> 00:41:03.600
– However, after defeating Ghostface number 9&nbsp;
and 10, Sidney and Gale decide not to ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:03.600 --> 00:41:06.720
speak publicly about the incident&nbsp;
so that the evil doers would die in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:06.720 --> 00:41:11.200
anonymity. Does this spell the end for the&nbsp;
Stab franchise? It will certainly be harder&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:11.200 --> 00:41:13.920
for the movies to remain relevant&nbsp;
without a real life connection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:13.920 --> 00:41:17.920
and without Gale to even write any fictional&nbsp;
stories upon which to base the movies.

00:41:17.920 --> 00:41:21.760
But if Stab and the movie industry has taught&nbsp;
us anything, it’s that as long as there’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:21.760 --> 00:41:27.360
a dime to be made off of the Stab IP, you can&nbsp;
probably expect more garbage to be shoveled out.

00:41:27.360 --> 00:41:29.920
At least we know Rian Johnson is getting work.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:30.407 --> 00:41:32.407
(impact)

00:41:32.847 --> 00:41:34.847
I want to give a thanks to Joey Noberini II.

00:41:34.848 --> 00:41:37.959
He archived all of those old Scream websites that are no longer online,  

00:41:37.959 --> 00:41:41.440
so this video would not have been fully possible if not for his contribution.

00:41:41.440 --> 00:41:45.600
If you want to learn more about the history of the&nbsp;
Scream franchise, head on over to that playlist on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:45.600 --> 00:41:49.520
the left, where I’ll be analyzing many characters&nbsp;
in the Scream canon. If you love horror,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:49.520 --> 00:41:53.600
make sure you subscribe to CZsWorld for new&nbsp;
horrors every week, ring the deathbell and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:41:53.600 --> 00:41:58.908
select all notifications and I’ll see you&nbsp;
in the next one. Assuming, we both survive.

00:41:58.908 --> 00:42:02.486
(spooky dance music)

